## The problem

The analytics already existed. Getting an answer out of them meant knowing where to
look, so questions queued up behind whoever knew the schema. Most of those questions
were small and repetitive, which is the worst kind of thing to be a bottleneck for.

## How it works

Company data is split into chunks and embedded with the OpenAI embeddings API, and the
vectors are stored so a question can be matched by similarity. At query time the
service embeds the question, pulls the passages that score highest, and passes only
those to the model as context. LangChain wires the steps together.

Retrieval quality mattered far more than prompt wording. Chunks that were too large
buried the relevant line in noise, and chunks that were too small lost the context
that made a number mean anything. Finding that boundary, and keeping metadata attached
to each chunk so the model could tell what it was looking at, is where most of the
time went.

## Keeping the answers honest

An assistant that invents a plausible number is worse than one that declines to
answer, because a plausible number gets used. Answers are grounded in the retrieved
passages and carry a reference back to the records they came from, so anyone can check
the work. When retrieval turns up nothing relevant, saying so is the correct output.

## Stack

Python and FastAPI, OpenAI embeddings for retrieval, LangChain for orchestration.
